    "note": "Enrolled text (Sub. S.B. 214, 136th GA, 05_EN, fetched 2026-11-16) replaces the earlier version read (02_PS, as passed by the Senate). The enrolled act ADDS removal of sawed-off firearms from dangerous ordnance (R.C. 2923.11 sawed-off definition struck; 'or sawed-off' struck in the dangerous-ordnance and sporting-weapon exception; matching cross-reference changes in R.C. 2923.12(C)(1)(c)-(d)); its long title now covers 2923.12. The enrolled act drops no part of the earlier version. Struck: in 1533.04(A) the proviso requiring lawful possession and National Firearms Act registration (the sentence's period is inside the struck run, so the quote ends at 'while hunting'); in 2923.11 the sawed-off definition, 'Any firearm muffler or suppressor' and the lettering shifts; in 2923.17(C) the exemption (8) for hunters with suppressors. Quoted dangerous-ordnance division lettering omitted because the print shows it as struck and re-lettered. Votes and sponsors as before (Open States bulk data and www.legislature.ohio.gov Votes pages)."
  },
  "operative_text": "Section 1 (R.C. 1533.04(A)): A person who holds a valid hunting license issued under this chapter and who hunts game birds or wild quadrupeds may use a suppressor attached to a gun that is authorized to be used for hunting by section 1533.16 of the Revised Code while hunting\nSection 1 (R.C. 2923.11): \"Dangerous ordnance\" means any of the following, except as provided in division (K) of this section: (1) Any automatic firearm, zip-gun, or ballistic knife; (2) Any explosive device or incendiary device;\nSection 1 (R.C. 2923.11): (5) Any combination of parts that is intended by the owner for use in converting any firearm or other device into a dangerous ordnance.\nSection 1 (R.C. 2923.11): (2) Any pistol, rifle, or shotgun, designed or suitable for sporting purposes, including a military weapon as issued or as modified, and the ammunition for that weapon, unless the firearm is an automatic firearm;\nSection 1 (R.C. 2923.17(A)): No person shall knowingly acquire, have, carry, or use any dangerous ordnance.\nSection 1 (R.C. 2923.17(D)): Whoever violates division (A) of this section is guilty of unlawful possession of dangerous ordnance, a felony of the fifth degree."
}